  "notes": "The Wu soul-maximalist, and it inverts two things every other Wu entry here does. First, key: \"major-key 70s soul as often as minor\", and because the samples are sped up the vocals ride high, so `major` leads the scale table and `melody.register` is the highest of the four Wu models. Second, mix balance: \"drums frequently *quieter* than the sample, sometimes near-absent on intros\" is the direct opposite of the 36 Chambers \"make it louder\" instruction, so the `velocityTiers` block is pushed **down** — accent tops out at 108 where the Wu-Tang entry starts at 112. That single inversion is what stops this reading as its own parent. The loops carry full band content with audible vocals, so `melody.densityPerBar` is 5–9 with `chordToneBias` high and real I–IV–V / ii–V–I motion; the horn stabs that answer the vocal are the countermelody. Verses run 20–32 bars without hooks on some cuts, which sets `sectionBars.verse` to 24, and mid-song beat switches put `switchUpProb` at 0.45. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it high-medium and says the idiom is documented at genre and album level rather than session level; the inferred parameters are the swing amount and the ghost-snare rate.",
